WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.001 --> 00:00:02.711
<i>It seems today ♪</i>

2
00:00:02.711 --> 00:00:04.587
<i>That all you see ♪</i>

3
00:00:04.671 --> 00:00:08.133
<i>Is violence in movies and sex on TV ♪</i>

4
00:00:08.133 --> 00:00:12.262
<i>♪ But where are those
good old-fashioned values ♪</i>

5
00:00:12.262 --> 00:00:14.389
<i>On which we used to rely? ♪</i>

6
00:00:14.389 --> 00:00:18.226
<i>Lucky there's a family guy ♪</i>

7
00:00:18.226 --> 00:00:21.521
<i>♪ Lucky there's a man who
positively can do ♪</i>

8
00:00:21.521 --> 00:00:23.106
<i>All the things that make us ♪</i>

9
00:00:23.106 --> 00:00:24.441
<i>Laugh and cry ♪</i>

10
00:00:24.441 --> 00:00:30.071
<i>He's... a... Fam... ily... Guy! ♪</i>

11
00:00:31.072 --> 00:00:32.741
{\an8}Hi, I'm Peter Griffin.

12
00:00:32.741 --> 00:00:34.576
{\an8}It's almost Oscar night.

13
00:00:34.576 --> 00:00:36.995
{\an8}Boy, we've lost so many greats this year,

14
00:00:36.995 --> 00:00:38.955
{\an8}including a dead costume designer

15
00:00:39.039 --> 00:00:40.540
{\an8}who will get spill-over applause

16
00:00:40.540 --> 00:00:43.543
{\an8}from the photo of Sean Connery
that directly precedes him.

17
00:00:43.543 --> 00:00:46.463
{\an8}Tonight, we salute
some past Best Picture winners,

18
00:00:46.463 --> 00:00:48.757
{\an8}whichever ones I spin on this wheel.

19
00:00:54.679 --> 00:00:56.473
{\an8}The wheel has spoken!

20
00:00:56.473 --> 00:00:58.183
{\an8}<i>The Silence of the Lambs.</i>

21
00:00:58.183 --> 00:01:00.268
{\an8}♪ ♪

22
00:01:04.773 --> 00:01:08.359
{\an8}Ah... what a beautiful day
to not get bodily fluids

23
00:01:08.443 --> 00:01:10.361
{\an8}thrown in your face and hair.

24
00:01:10.445 --> 00:01:14.657
{\an8}And now, for some rock tunes
to pump me up.

25
00:01:14.741 --> 00:01:17.077
{\an8} <i>One, two princes kneel before you... ♪</i>

26
00:01:17.077 --> 00:01:20.413
{\an8}Oh, right, 'cause it's the early '90s.

27
00:01:20.497 --> 00:01:22.332
{\an8}Vaulting over a log.

28
00:01:22.332 --> 00:01:24.084
{\an8}Jumping a water hazard.

29
00:01:24.084 --> 00:01:26.336
{\an8}Climbing up a cargo net.

30
00:01:26.336 --> 00:01:28.379
{\an8}Becoming an FBI agent is basically

31
00:01:28.463 --> 00:01:30.924
{\an8}like being on <i>Battle of the Network Stars.</i>

32
00:01:30.924 --> 00:01:33.551
{\an8}- <i>You rockets ain't in... ♪</i>
-(music stops)

33
00:01:33.635 --> 00:01:35.637
{\an8}♪ ♪

34
00:01:38.389 --> 00:01:40.391
{\an8}- Aah!
- Agent Starling.

35
00:01:40.475 --> 00:01:41.601
{\an8}Good to see you.

36
00:01:41.601 --> 00:01:43.353
{\an8}Wh-Why is your face so big?

37
00:01:43.353 --> 00:01:45.855
{\an8}Oh, this movie
is mostly extreme close-ups.

38
00:01:45.939 --> 00:01:47.816
{\an8}Oh. Yeah. Weird.

39
00:01:47.816 --> 00:01:49.776
{\an8}Anyway, sorry you had to come so quickly,

40
00:01:49.776 --> 00:01:52.153
{\an8}but this thing with no timetable
couldn't wait.

41
00:01:52.237 --> 00:01:55.115
{\an8}We need you to talk
to a super smart psychopath.

42
00:01:55.115 --> 00:01:56.574
{\an8}How smart?

43
00:01:56.658 --> 00:01:58.076
{\an8}Like, he can do
the <i>New York Times</i> crossword

44
00:01:58.076 --> 00:01:59.244
{\an8}through Thursday.

45
00:01:59.244 --> 00:02:01.412
{\an8}Friday, he can maybe get a corner or two.

46
00:02:01.496 --> 00:02:03.081
{\an8}So, what's the job?

47
00:02:03.081 --> 00:02:04.666
{\an8}Fancy creep, eats people.

48
00:02:04.666 --> 00:02:06.543
{\an8}- Talk to him.
- Got it.

49
00:02:06.543 --> 00:02:08.461
{\an8}And this is a big deal,
'cause you're a woman.

50
00:02:08.545 --> 00:02:10.171
{\an8}We're not gonna say it's a big deal,

51
00:02:10.255 --> 00:02:12.465
{\an8}but everyone watching
will know it's a big deal

52
00:02:12.549 --> 00:02:13.758
{\an8}'cause of the time period,

53
00:02:13.842 --> 00:02:15.343
{\an8}even though a man is helping you.

54
00:02:15.343 --> 00:02:16.803
{\an8}Now run along, toots.

55
00:02:17.804 --> 00:02:19.806
{\an8}(bird cawing)

56
00:02:20.807 --> 00:02:22.475
{\an8}(yelling gibberish)

57
00:02:22.559 --> 00:02:24.269
{\an8}So, you're in the FBI, huh?

58
00:02:24.269 --> 00:02:26.104
{\an8}Me too.

59
00:02:26.104 --> 00:02:28.481
{\an8}Got this bad boy in Venice Beach.

60
00:02:30.066 --> 00:02:31.943
{\an8}Okay, all right.

61
00:02:31.943 --> 00:02:33.653
{\an8}You can talk to Lecter now.

62
00:02:33.653 --> 00:02:36.489
{\an8}♪ ♪

63
00:02:40.493 --> 00:02:42.495
{\an8}Hello, Clarice.

64
00:02:42.579 --> 00:02:44.831
{\an8}Dr. Lecter, I need your help.

65
00:02:44.831 --> 00:02:47.917
{\an8}Nope, nope, nope.
I'll only help if there's a quid pro quo.

66
00:02:48.001 --> 00:02:49.711
{\an8}(sighs) Okay, what is it?

67
00:02:49.711 --> 00:02:53.840
{\an8}I want Mario Lopez to record
my outgoing voice mail message.

68
00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:55.675
{\an8}What?! You're insane!

69
00:02:55.675 --> 00:02:59.012
{\an8}He's a huge star
at this specific sliver of time.

70
00:02:59.012 --> 00:03:01.598
{\an8}Figure it out. Those are my terms.

71
00:03:01.598 --> 00:03:03.683
{\an8}♪ ♪

72
00:03:05.143 --> 00:03:06.644
{\an8}(gasps)

73
00:03:06.728 --> 00:03:10.648
{\an8}Sorry, these Capri Suns
are notoriously difficult to open.

74
00:03:10.732 --> 00:03:12.650
{\an8}(Spin Doctors' "Two Princes"
playing on radio)

75
00:03:12.734 --> 00:03:15.737
{\an8} <i>Yeah, one, two... ♪</i>

76
00:03:17.155 --> 00:03:18.990
{\an8}(faintly): <i>That's what I said now ♪</i>

77
00:03:18.990 --> 00:03:20.450
{\an8}- <i>Princes... ♪</i>
-(music stops)

78
00:03:24.913 --> 00:03:26.497
{\an8}(straining)

79
00:03:27.498 --> 00:03:28.833
{\an8}You need some help?

80
00:03:28.917 --> 00:03:30.084
{\an8}Yeah, thanks.

81
00:03:30.168 --> 00:03:33.504
{\an8}I can't seem to get
this giant 1991 computer

82
00:03:33.588 --> 00:03:35.673
{\an8}into the back of my van.

83
00:03:35.757 --> 00:03:39.594
{\an8}Careful, I had it on
for 20 minutes, so it's very hot.

84
00:03:39.594 --> 00:03:40.803
{\an8}(grunts)

85
00:03:44.933 --> 00:03:47.185
{\an8}Okay, I got what you asked for.

86
00:03:47.185 --> 00:03:48.853
{\an8}<i>MARIO LOPEZ: Hey, this is Mario Lopez</i>

87
00:03:48.937 --> 00:03:50.355
{\an8}<i>letting you know that Hannibal Lecter</i>

88
00:03:50.355 --> 00:03:51.814
{\an8}<i>can't get to the phone right now,</i>

89
00:03:51.898 --> 00:03:53.441
{\an8}<i>so please leave a message.</i>

90
00:03:53.441 --> 00:03:55.235
{\an8}<i>And now, I think
I'm about to be saved by the...</i>

91
00:03:55.235 --> 00:03:56.361
{\an8}(beep)

92
00:03:56.361 --> 00:03:57.570
Not good enough!

93
00:03:57.654 --> 00:03:59.447
I wanted him to record it as Slater.

94
00:03:59.447 --> 00:04:01.532
What?! You never said that.

95
00:04:01.616 --> 00:04:03.117
It should've been understood.

96
00:04:03.201 --> 00:04:04.827
Don't test me, Starling.

97
00:04:04.911 --> 00:04:06.621
A census taker did that once,

98
00:04:06.621 --> 00:04:09.040
and I ate his liver with dry Cheerios

99
00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:11.042
and a mashed-up banana.

100
00:04:11.042 --> 00:04:13.002
(slurping)

101
00:04:13.086 --> 00:04:15.463
Sorry, I'm just a little nervous.

102
00:04:15.463 --> 00:04:17.173
When I was a girl,

103
00:04:17.257 --> 00:04:18.591
I went to live on my uncle's farm,

104
00:04:18.675 --> 00:04:21.803
and I heard these lambs screaming,

105
00:04:21.803 --> 00:04:23.554
screaming as they were being slaughtered.

106
00:04:23.638 --> 00:04:25.473
It was horrible.

107
00:04:25.473 --> 00:04:28.268
-(applause)
- And I want to thank
Alan Weiss at William Morris.

108
00:04:28.268 --> 00:04:31.271
Also, my high school drama teacher,
Lucas Wohl.

109
00:04:31.271 --> 00:04:33.982
-(orchestral music plays)
- Oh, they were just
killing those poor lambs.

110
00:04:33.982 --> 00:04:35.316
I wanted to save them, but I couldn't.

111
00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:38.194
And, Mandy and Jenny
at home, go to bed, kids.

112
00:04:38.278 --> 00:04:39.821
Mommy did it!

113
00:04:39.821 --> 00:04:42.073
I am coming home with one of these!

114
00:04:42.073 --> 00:04:44.409
-(laughing)
-(applause)

115
00:04:44.409 --> 00:04:47.412
♪ ♪

116
00:04:50.999 --> 00:04:53.501
CVS brand lotion?

117
00:04:53.501 --> 00:04:56.170
It's the same ingredients as Nivea.

118
00:04:57.255 --> 00:05:00.133
It puts the off-brand lotion on its skin,

119
00:05:00.133 --> 00:05:02.844
or else it gets the hose again.

120
00:05:02.844 --> 00:05:04.595
The top isn't even a pump.

121
00:05:04.679 --> 00:05:06.848
You're paying for the pump!

122
00:05:06.848 --> 00:05:09.475
♪ ♪

123
00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:15.857
Dr. Lecter, we are running out of time.

124
00:05:15.857 --> 00:05:17.775
I need you to give me some clue,

125
00:05:17.859 --> 00:05:20.194
anything that might
lead me in the right direction.

126
00:05:20.278 --> 00:05:23.239
(sighs) His name's Buffalo Bill,
heavyset blond kid

127
00:05:23.323 --> 00:05:25.742
who lives in that crappy place
behind McDonald's.

128
00:05:25.742 --> 00:05:27.160
Hmm.

129
00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:28.453
I'll have to really lean

130
00:05:28.453 --> 00:05:30.455
on my budding criminology skills

131
00:05:30.455 --> 00:05:33.082
to decipher these obscure breadcrumbs.

132
00:05:33.166 --> 00:05:35.585
♪ ♪

133
00:06:03.988 --> 00:06:05.990
(gasps slowly)

134
00:06:12.163 --> 00:06:13.748
Coming up, dinner.

135
00:06:13.748 --> 00:06:16.626
But first, I turn my back
to a dangerous murderer.

136
00:06:17.627 --> 00:06:19.420
(regurgitates)

137
00:06:23.883 --> 00:06:26.677
(indistinct chatter)

138
00:06:28.888 --> 00:06:31.391
{\an8}Oh, hey, Sarge.
Your wife called and said it was urgent.

139
00:06:31.391 --> 00:06:33.226
{\an8}Oh, uh, okay.

140
00:06:33.226 --> 00:06:35.937
Yeah, she sounded very not happy.

141
00:06:35.937 --> 00:06:37.605
You need to call her back ASAP.

142
00:06:37.605 --> 00:06:38.815
Got it, thanks.

143
00:06:38.815 --> 00:06:41.651
Starting to think I did this guy a favor.

144
00:06:48.449 --> 00:06:51.494
It puts its finger on the doorbell.

145
00:06:51.494 --> 00:06:53.037
We're looking for someone.

146
00:06:53.121 --> 00:06:54.497
Have you seen her?

147
00:06:54.497 --> 00:06:58.626
Oh, wait, is she that
great, big, huge, plump,

148
00:06:58.626 --> 00:07:02.004
very large, Rubenesque,
fat girl who went missing?

149
00:07:02.088 --> 00:07:03.506
Yes, she is rather heavy,

150
00:07:03.506 --> 00:07:05.883
and as you can see,
not particularly attractive.

151
00:07:05.967 --> 00:07:07.718
We've had reports that her personality

152
00:07:07.802 --> 00:07:09.262
is quite grating as well.

153
00:07:09.262 --> 00:07:11.472
MEG (distant): I can still hear you guys!

154
00:07:15.768 --> 00:07:17.770
(panting)

155
00:07:28.781 --> 00:07:30.366
(gasps)

156
00:07:31.492 --> 00:07:33.828
I'm not loving this night vision setting.

157
00:07:33.828 --> 00:07:37.790
Maybe I should
try <i>Night at the Roxbury</i> vision.

158
00:07:37.874 --> 00:07:39.917
(Haddaway's "What Is Love?" playing)

159
00:07:40.001 --> 00:07:42.295
♪ ♪

160
00:07:43.796 --> 00:07:46.257
- You? Me? You?
- <i>What is love? ♪</i>
- Aah!

161
00:07:47.925 --> 00:07:51.554
It puts the bullet in the fat guy.

162
00:07:55.558 --> 00:07:58.060
And, for having successfully
scaled a cargo net

163
00:07:58.144 --> 00:08:01.522
and connecting two note cards
with a piece of red string,

164
00:08:01.606 --> 00:08:06.152
Clarice Starling,
you are now officially a lady FBI.

165
00:08:06.152 --> 00:08:08.654
(applause)

166
00:08:10.573 --> 00:08:12.575
(phone ringing)

167
00:08:16.162 --> 00:08:17.538
You should probably answer that.

168
00:08:17.622 --> 00:08:20.082
I mean, you're the closest thing
to a secretary in here.

169
00:08:21.083 --> 00:08:22.793
Ugh.

170
00:08:22.877 --> 00:08:26.339
Hello, FBI. This is Clarice speaking.

171
00:08:26.339 --> 00:08:27.882
Hello, Clarice.

172
00:08:27.882 --> 00:08:30.468
I just wanted to let you know
that I respect you,

173
00:08:30.468 --> 00:08:32.929
and how important it is
that you're a woman.

174
00:08:32.929 --> 00:08:35.181
Thank you for saying so, Doctor.

175
00:08:35.181 --> 00:08:36.516
'Cause if you were a man,

176
00:08:36.516 --> 00:08:38.768
I would definitely
be captured or dead by now.

177
00:08:38.768 --> 00:08:41.312
- Oh, I...
- Like, if you're Clint Eastwood, I'm dead.

178
00:08:41.312 --> 00:08:44.023
- Harrison Ford? Dead.
- Well, Doctor, I've really got...

179
00:08:44.023 --> 00:08:46.234
You know, I'm not betting
against the <i>Twin Peaks</i> guy--

180
00:08:46.234 --> 00:08:47.777
what's his name? Creepy white guy.

181
00:08:47.777 --> 00:08:50.279
- Kyle MacLachlan.
- Kyle MacLachlan, right.

182
00:08:50.363 --> 00:08:51.531
How do I keep forgetting that?

183
00:08:51.531 --> 00:08:53.866
It's like in my top five dude names.
I have a list.

184
00:08:53.950 --> 00:08:56.494
- Dr. Lecter, I really have to go.
- So do I.

185
00:08:56.494 --> 00:08:58.579
♪ ♪

186
00:09:00.248 --> 00:09:03.084
I'm having an old friend for dinner.

187
00:09:03.084 --> 00:09:04.919
Y-You're gonna eat a friend?

188
00:09:04.919 --> 00:09:06.504
That was kind of
supposed to be the last line,

189
00:09:06.504 --> 00:09:07.755
and you ruined it.

190
00:09:07.755 --> 00:09:09.674
I'm gonna say it again
and then hang up quickly.

191
00:09:09.674 --> 00:09:10.967
(clears throat)

192
00:09:10.967 --> 00:09:13.135
I'm having an old friend for dinner.

193
00:09:13.219 --> 00:09:14.178
<i>What?!</i>

194
00:09:14.262 --> 00:09:16.055
Oh, my God, this bitch.

195
00:09:18.516 --> 00:09:20.518
♪ ♪

196
00:09:21.561 --> 00:09:23.854
<i>PETER: My name is...</i>

197
00:09:23.938 --> 00:09:26.649
<i>whatever Kevin Spacey's name was
in this movie.</i>

198
00:09:26.649 --> 00:09:28.526
<i>I bet you think behind that door is</i>

199
00:09:28.526 --> 00:09:30.152
<i>a happy, all-American family.</i>

200
00:09:30.236 --> 00:09:31.153
<i>Nope.</i>

201
00:09:31.237 --> 00:09:32.530
<i>Just me in the shower</i>

202
00:09:32.530 --> 00:09:34.532
<i>with Johnson & Johnson and Johnson.</i>

203
00:09:34.532 --> 00:09:36.742
<i>There were some tears.</i>

204
00:09:36.826 --> 00:09:39.078
<i>That's my wife out there, cutting roses.</i>

205
00:09:39.078 --> 00:09:41.831
<i>Oh, just a heads-up,
there's a ton of roses in this.</i>

206
00:09:41.831 --> 00:09:44.375
<i>I guess they're
supposed to mean something. (sighs)</i>

207
00:09:44.375 --> 00:09:45.793
<i>You know, when I agreed to this,</i>

208
00:09:45.793 --> 00:09:48.004
<i>I 100% thought it was</i> American Pie.

209
00:09:48.004 --> 00:09:50.089
<i>Always read the second word of the title</i>

210
00:09:50.089 --> 00:09:51.424
<i>before you sign something.</i>

211
00:09:51.424 --> 00:09:54.218
Oh, hi, Carolyn. We're your gay neighbors.

212
00:09:54.302 --> 00:09:56.137
That's right, and we're
the only happy people

213
00:09:56.137 --> 00:09:57.638
in the entire film.

214
00:09:57.722 --> 00:09:58.889
<i>PETER: Oh, that's the other thing.</i>

215
00:09:58.973 --> 00:10:00.558
<i>This movie really wants you to know</i>

216
00:10:00.558 --> 00:10:03.185
<i>that traditional families
are the messed-up ones.</i>

217
00:10:03.269 --> 00:10:04.687
<i>If you don't want two hours of that,</i>

218
00:10:04.687 --> 00:10:06.272
<i>go turn on</i> The Matrix.

219
00:10:06.272 --> 00:10:07.857
<i>Aw, why aren't we doing that?</i>

220
00:10:07.857 --> 00:10:09.191
Have a great day,

221
00:10:09.275 --> 00:10:11.485
two-dimensional '90s gay neighbors.

222
00:10:11.569 --> 00:10:14.864
- Who are both named Jim.
- Right, who are both named Jim.

223
00:10:15.948 --> 00:10:18.534
- Good morning, my darling.
- Hello, my love.

224
00:10:18.618 --> 00:10:20.870
<i>PETER: Keep in mind
that 9/11 hasn't happened yet.</i>

225
00:10:20.870 --> 00:10:22.955
<i>So a strained family dynamic</i>

226
00:10:23.039 --> 00:10:25.166
<i>is still the most dramatic thing there is.</i>

227
00:10:25.166 --> 00:10:28.878
Wow, what a beautiful,
fully heterosexual neighborhood.

228
00:10:28.878 --> 00:10:31.797
Yeah, no. What's up is down here, bro.

229
00:10:31.881 --> 00:10:34.258
Oh, hi, you must be our new neighbors.

230
00:10:34.342 --> 00:10:36.093
I'm Carolyn Burnham,

231
00:10:36.177 --> 00:10:39.055
RE/MAX's top crying realtor in the area.

232
00:10:39.055 --> 00:10:40.973
Uh, thanks, but we just bought a house,

233
00:10:41.057 --> 00:10:42.808
so we don't really need a realtor.

234
00:10:42.892 --> 00:10:45.519
(crying)

235
00:10:45.603 --> 00:10:47.688
- You don't?
- S-Sorry.

236
00:10:47.772 --> 00:10:49.523
It's okay. You know what?

237
00:10:49.607 --> 00:10:50.816
We'll buy another one.

238
00:10:50.900 --> 00:10:53.152
Oh, great. Great.

239
00:10:53.152 --> 00:10:55.780
Oh, looks like your son and my daughter

240
00:10:55.780 --> 00:10:57.281
are getting to know each other.

241
00:10:57.365 --> 00:11:00.576
